## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates designing scalable, secure, cross-account data access across tenants with strong isolation and auditable sharing.

## Key Concepts
- Lake Formation catalog-based permissions
- S3 per-tenant prefixes and object ownership
- KMS key policies for residency and encryption
- IAM SCPs and cross-account sharing, data marts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tenant user policy (pseudo)
{
  'Version': '2012-10-17',
  'Statement': [
    {'Effect': 'Allow', 'Action': ['lakeformation:GrantPermissions', 's3:GetObject'], 'Resource': ['arn:aws:s3:::corp-data/tenant-id-*/**']}
  ]
}

// Data-mart role policy (pseudo)
{
  'Version': '2012-10-17',
  'Statement': [
    {'Effect': 'Allow', 'Action': ['lakeformation:SelectFromDataCatalog'], 'Resource': '*'}
  ]
}

// Cross-account share policy (pseudo)
{
  'Version': '2012-10-17',
  'Statement': [
    {'Effect': 'Allow', 'Action': ['glue:GetTable','s3:GetObject'], 'Resource': ['arn:aws:glue:*:*:catalog','arn:aws:s3:::shared-data/**']}
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor and alert on policy drift?
- What are the trade-offs of using Lake Formation versus direct IAM policies for data access?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical serverless wiring, least-privilege IAM, and cross-account access basics.

## Key Concepts
- Lambda IAM roles, API Gateway integration, S3 PutObject, SSE-KMS, cross-account roles.
- KMS key policies and bucket policies.
- End-to-end testing of write and cross-account read.

## Code Example
```javascript
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const s3 = new AWS.S3();
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const payload = JSON.stringify(event);
  await s3.putObject({
    Bucket: process.env.LOG_BUCKET,
    Key: `logs/${Date.now()}.json`,
    Body: payload,
    ServerSideEncryption: 'aws:kms'
  }).promise();
  return { statusCode: 200, body: 'ok' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test failure modes (kms failure, bucket policy denial)?
- How would you rotate the KMS key and propagate changes?
